<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer used a three-day Gulf tour, along with a wider diplomatic push across Europe and the Middle East, to strengthen ties with other partners as his relationship with President Trump has grown more strained. The shift follows Trump’s renewed criticism of NATO and his threat to pull the United States out of the alliance after other members refused to join the US-Israel war against Iran. Starmer, by contrast, publicly argued that NATO still serves America’s interests as well as Europe’s. Starmer said he used a phone call with Trump to pass along Gulf states’ views and discuss a practical plan to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, which remains largely closed despite a temporary US-Iran ceasefire announced this week. The outreach suggests Britain is trying to keep working with Washington while also broadening its network of partners during a fragile regional truce, a sign that London is looking for steadier diplomatic options in a volatile moment.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ia-7fTQjUvh_fMODdCseyFpmeHP2Q3GuHuvuFAYjvX-unZ4dZ-t-4iiOzFA9sPpuWE9l85r2Ol5YhSwDIZ86wPuCLQMXZoI2V5IwE_eRyfqF' alt='Britain’s Prime Minister Keir Starmer speaks to the media at the airport in Doha, Qatar, April 10, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Starmer’s Gulf trip took him to Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Qatar, with Doha as the final stop before he returned to Britain.</li><li>Britain is building a 30-nation coalition around a “practical plan” to reopen the Strait of Hormuz after recent disruptions, Starmer said.</li><li>Starmer defended NATO while also calling on European members to carry more of the load, signaling support for the alliance while acknowledging US complaints about spending.</li><li>The temporary ceasefire between the United States and Iran remains fragile, with Israeli attacks on Lebanon and disputes over whether the truce is being carried out putting it at risk.</li></ul><blockquote>It is in America’s interests. It’s in European interests. - Keir Starmer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Keir Starmer: Starmer said NATO is a defensive alliance that has helped keep Europe safer for decades, while also arguing that European members need to contribute more financially. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990663/starmer-says-nato-in-americas-interests-as-gulf-tour-ends'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump renewed his attacks on NATO and threatened to pull the United States out after allies refused to join the US-Israel war against Iran. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/starmer-says-nato-uss-interests-gulf-tour-ends'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>New York Times analysis: The newspaper said Starmer’s frustration with Trump has pushed him to deepen outreach to European and Middle Eastern partners as the UK-US relationship cools. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/10/world/europe/trump-starmer-fed-up-iran-war.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>WSWS analysis: The outlet described Starmer’s Gulf trip as a sign that long-standing assumptions behind British foreign policy and the “special relationship” with Washington are under strain. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/04/10/ditt-a10.html'>World Socialist Web Site</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/10/world/europe/trump-starmer-fed-up-iran-war.html'>‘I’m Fed Up.’ Frustrated With Trump, Starmer Embraces Other Allies.</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990663/starmer-says-nato-in-americas-interests-as-gulf-tour-ends'>Starmer says Nato in America’s ‘interests’ as Gulf tour ends</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/im-fed-up-frustrated-with-trump-britains-starmer-embraces-other-allies'>‘I’m fed up’: Frustrated with Trump, Britain’s Starmer embraces other allies</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/starmer-says-nato-uss-interests-gulf-tour-ends'>Starmer says NATO in US&#x27;s &#x27;interests&#x27; as Gulf tour ends</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/04/10/ditt-a10.html'>Starmer visits Gulf states amid fracturing “special relationship” with US</a> - wsws.org</li></ul>